{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusades and the history of the County of Tripoli. He inherited the county at a young age succeeding his father Raymond II. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and efforts to consolidate the county's power against various enemies including Muslim forces and rival Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III's early life was shaped by the ongoing Crusades and the political instability of the Levant. He received military training and was involved in campaigns led by his father. Upon Raymond II's death in 1155 Raymond III ascended to the county facing immediate challenges to his rule. These challenges included internal dissent and external threats from the Zengid dynasty a powerful Muslim force in the region. He skillfully navigated these difficulties establishing his authority and securing the county's borders. His early years were dedicated to solidifying his position and maintaining stability within Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed significant military activity. He engaged in numerous battles against the Zengids and other Muslim rulers seeking to maintain the county's independence. He allied with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch forming strategic partnerships to counter shared threats. His military campaigns often involved sieges raids and skirmishes aimed at disrupting enemy forces and securing key territories. He also focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses building fortifications and maintaining a well-equipped army.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a shrewd diplomat adept at forging alliances and navigating complex political relationships. He maintained a delicate balance of power engaging in negotiations and treaties with both Muslim rulers and 